// A METHOD DECLARING TYPE PARAMETERS OF ITS OWN — `tag = <U>(self, u: U)`
// — IS REFUSED AT THE CALL, and this is the file that says so out loud.
// `gen_c_member.method_fault` states the rule: a generic RECEIVER is not
// a refusal, because `Vec<GenDiag>.add` is `Vec.add` emitted at
// `GenDiag` and the receiver's arguments say which; a member's OWN
// parameters would need the receiver's arguments plus the call's, "and
// nothing in this tree declares one".
//
// Which is exactly why it needs a test here rather than in the corpus.
// The refusal is load-bearing and invisible: `src/` never writes the
// shape, so the guard fires nowhere the build can see it, and a check
// that fires nowhere reads the same as a check that was deleted. If the
// day comes that generic methods lower, this file goes red and the
// corpus test replacing it is the one to write.
//
// The guard is also the only thing keeping the report readable. Removed,
// the program does not compile either — it fails further down as
// "codegen cannot spell the type" at the two declarations, which names the
// MEMBER and never the call that asked for it, and the three call sites
// go unmentioned. That is the difference this file is holding: a
// refusal that points at what the author wrote.
//
// THE DECLARATION IS NOT THE ERROR. Sema types the shape and says
// nothing about it; `Box` below is constructed and read, and only the
// CALLS are named. Three forms, three positions: the type argument
// inferred from the argument, the type argument written, and the same
// on a `::=` member, which reaches the refusal by the same door.

Box = {
    n :: i32,
    tag = <U>(self: @Self, u: U) i32 { self.n; }
    bump ::= <U>(self :: @Self, u: U) i32 { self.n = self.n + 1; self.n; }
}

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    b ::= Box(n: 4);
    println("{}", b.n);
    println("{}", b.tag(9));
    println("{}", b.tag<i32>(9));
    println("{}", b.bump(9));
    Ok(0);
}
